earth: a line you will not cross is put to a price: you said no once, and the money comes back larger, with a word of regret
times: A bigger sum for a sacred value tends to harden refusal, while an apology or a word of respect softens it (Ginges et al. 2007)
gap: 3-8
scene: {N} has already said no once. Now the offer comes back larger, carried by someone more senior, with a word of regret for how the first approach was made. The offer is put plainly: {offer}. The sum has grown, and so has the sense that a second no will be remembered.
scene W: The regret sounds decent and the sum would see the family right, and {N} has to ask whether pride is all that is still saying no.
